Day 3 — Processes, Storage and Networking

1. Process Management

- "top" — Monitor running processes interactively. Press "q" to exit.
- "ps" — Display process information.
- "ps aux" — Display detailed information about running processes.

2. Running Commands in the Background

nohup command > output.log 2>&1 &

- "nohup" — Helps a command continue after terminal logout.
- ">" — Redirects output to a file.
- "2>&1" — Redirects error output to the same destination.
- "&" — Starts the command in the background.

Replace "command" with the program you want to run.

3. Storage and Memory

- "df -h" — Show filesystem disk space.
- "free -h" — Show memory usage.

The "-h" option displays sizes in human-readable units.

4. Downloading Files

wget "URL"

Downloads a file from a URL. Replace "URL" with the actual download address.

5. ZIP Compression

zip archive.zip file.txt
unzip archive.zip

- "zip" — Creates a ZIP archive.
- "unzip" — Extracts a ZIP archive.

6. TAR Archives

tar -czvf archive.tar.gz folder/
tar -xzvf archive.tar.gz

- "-c" — Create an archive.
- "-x" — Extract an archive.
- "-z" — Use gzip compression.
- "-v" — Display processed files.
- "-f" — Specify the archive filename.

7. Remote Connections

SSH:

ssh username@hostname

Connects securely to a remote computer.

SCP:

scp file.txt username@hostname:/path/

Copies a file securely to a remote computer.

These require an accessible remote computer and suitable authentication.

8. Network Information

ifconfig

Displays network interface information if the utility is installed. On modern Linux systems, "ip addr" is a common alternative.


